Simple laceration from domestic animal scratch to dorsal hand
Minor Trauma and Personal Injury
Parallel linear skin tears, Immediate burning erythema, Domestic animal swipe mechanism

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MTI-019
Reason for Visit: Acute painful animal scratches to the hand requiring urgent decontamination and assessment for prophylactic antibiotics.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and pet cat
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported an acute feline scratch to the dorsal hand breaking the skin. Urgent clinical evaluation was necessary to execute high-volume decontamination, evaluate for tendon exposure, and initiate early targeted antimicrobial prophylaxis to prevent rapidly advancing Pasteurella or Bartonella cellulitis prior to the next daytime clinic.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My cat swiped my hand and the scratches are burning, red, and puffing up.
History of Present Illness: Patient provides a clear history of a defensive swipe from a domestic cat occurring two hours ago. This resulted in superficial linear tearing of the skin on the dorsal hand, presenting as multiple parallel tracts. The patient reports sharp burning pain that peaked immediately following the physical insult. A localized erythematous wheal formation surrounds the abrasions due to a histamine response. There is minimal oozing of capillary blood that spontaneously coagulated. Patient denies any deep puncture bites.
Functional Status: Fully independent; full use of the hand but uncomfortable.
Social/Safety Concerns: Animal behavior should be monitored, but no immediate environmental risk.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 122/76 | HR: 82 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Alert, well-perfused, frequently rubbing the area proximal to the scratches.
Physical Exam: Hand examination reveals parallel linear skin tears over the dorsal aspect. There is immediate burning erythema and localized wheal formation. Direct clinical visualization confirms the wounds are superficial without deep puncture components that typify an envenomated bite. Intact underlying extensor tendon function is demonstrated by full, pain-free active finger extension against resistance. Normal two-point discrimination distal to the injury indicates intact sensory nerves. There is a complete lack of rapidly spreading purulent discharge or deep space fluctuance in the acute phase.
Environmental Observations: Pet cat present, appears healthy and vaccinated per owner.
Medication Reconciliation: No routine medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Simple laceration (scratch) from domestic animal to dorsal hand - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Animal bite puncture wound - Ruled out due to: Visual confirmation of linear, superficial dermo-epidermal tears rather than deep, narrow puncture tracts.

Extensor tendon laceration - Ruled out due to: Intact active finger extension against resistance without pain or weakness.

Acute cellulitis (Pasteurella multocida) - Ruled out due to: Early presentation (<3 hours) without spreading induration, purulence, or systemic fever; erythema is currently limited to a localized histamine reaction.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

Animal-inflicted wounds, particularly feline scratches to the hand, carry a notoriously high risk of aggressive secondary bacterial infection involving deep fascial planes. On-site assessment confirmed superficial, parallel linear abrasions without structural tendon damage or deep puncture. Urgent after-hours assessment was clinically mandated to perform immediate, thorough mechanical decontamination and prescribe prophylactic oral antibiotics to preemptively target Pasteurella species, thereby averting a rapidly progressive hand infection that would likely develop if treatment were delayed until morning.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Performed thorough mechanical irrigation with sterile saline and chlorhexidine solution. Prescribed a 3-to-5 day prophylactic course of oral Amoxicillin/Clavulanate due to high-risk location (hand) and feline mechanism. Tetanus status up to date.
Positive Behavioral Change: Complete the entire course of prophylactic antibiotics exactly as prescribed, even if the hand looks completely healed. This maintains adequate tissue drug levels to completely eradicate latent bacteria and strictly prevents a delayed, severe joint space infection.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Simple adhesive dressings.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 48 hours for a mandatory wound check to ensure no early signs of cellulitis are developing.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the Emergency Department immediately if the redness rapidly spreads up your arm, if you lose the ability to straighten your fingers, or if thick pus begins draining from the scratches.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Cat scratches frequently harbor bacteria that can cause severe hand infections; strict monitoring is essential over the next 48 hours.
Clinical Course: The sharp stinging will subside by tomorrow. The scratches will form thin scabs and heal over 7-10 days.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical care if your hand becomes severely swollen, stiff, throbbing, or if you develop a fever and chills.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Cat claws act like dirty scalpels, pushing dangerous bacteria under the skin. The antibiotics directly target these specific bacteria before they can multiply and spread.
Actionable Steps: Take your antibiotics with food. Keep the scratches clean and dry. Watch closely for any red streaks tracking away from the wound.

